Most AI assistants answer questions. Computer use is different: the assistant operates your apps for you. On 19 November 2026 Google announced that this is rolling out in the Gemini app for Mac. This post reads Google's announcement and the matching Gemini Apps Help page and sets out what is stated, what is restricted and what the pages leave unsaid.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Google is a competitor of Anthropic. Stackbrook did not test Gemini or any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Google announced

According to Google's post, computer use lets Gemini carry out repetitive chores across the apps on your Mac. The post says Gemini works inside an isolated background window, with a live preview shown in your chat, so, as the post frames it, the task does not take over your screen. Google describes it as rolling out in English in the US.

You start a task from the Gemini app by choosing Task below the chat box and mentioning @Computer use, as the help page describes it. You can open the preview full screen, and a Stop task button ends the run.

## Who can use it

The eligibility rules are narrow, and the two pages mostly agree.

| Requirement | What Google states |
|---|---|
| Account | Personal Google account, age 18 or over; the help page says work or school accounts are not supported |
| Plan | Google AI Plus, Pro or Ultra |
| Region and language | English, United States |
| Computer | Apple Silicon Mac running macOS Sequoia 15.0 or later (stated in the blog post footnote) |

The help page also suggests choosing a recent Gemini model for better quality responses. The blog post does not mention that, and we did not find usage limits or any extra price beyond the subscription tiers on either page, and neither page says whether other countries or languages are planned.

![Flow diagram of the setup and start steps summarised from Google's help page for Gemini computer use on a Mac](/images/site1/gemini-computer-use-mac-explained-what-google-says-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

## How access and approvals work

The help page walks through setup. In the Gemini app on your Mac, open Settings and find Computer use, then check that the option allowing Gemini to work with your Mac is on. If it is not, the page says Accessibility and Screen and System Audio Recording permissions need to be turned on. It also tells you to turn on on-demand file backups.

Approvals happen per app. The first time Gemini needs an app, it asks whether to deny, allow once or always allow. Allow once and deny apply only to that step or chat, while always allow adds the app to an Allowed apps list. You can also add apps to a Blocked apps list and remove entries later. For sensitive tasks, Google says Gemini will ask for confirmation before it continues, and its blog post says you can pause at any time and approve key actions.

## What can go wrong

The help page lists several risks, and it is worth reading before you switch this on. Google says Gemini can make mistakes, that some actions cannot be undone, and that it can reach accounts already signed in within native Mac apps. It warns that content on screen can steer Gemini across app boundaries, that granting access to editors or developer tools can enable shell commands, and that Gemini can see or interact with multi-factor codes displayed on screen. It adds that automating some third-party apps may conflict with their terms of service, and that you are responsible for choosing those apps.

Backups are best effort. Google says Gemini tries to make a temporary backup but is not always able to, keeps backups for 24 hours or until your next prompt, and that some apps may not be backed up. That means a backup is a convenience, not a guarantee.

## Questions to ask before trying it, if you are eligible

This is general information, not security or legal advice. If you do qualify, Google's own warnings suggest some questions. Do you want to grant each app separately rather than always allow it? Do you want shell-level reach through a code editor? Are you watching the live preview during a task? Stackbrook's own suggestion, not Google guidance, is to start with low-stakes chores and to think carefully before giving access to apps that hold sensitive accounts. None of this has been tested by Stackbrook.

## What is still unknown

We did not find a statement of how to disable the feature entirely, what usage limits apply, or when it will reach other regions on either page. No independent testing was reviewed for this post, so how reliably it completes real tasks is unconfirmed. The honest summary is a US-only, subscriber-only agent feature with visible controls and a long list of vendor-stated risks.
